      <h1>New Spay, Neuter, and Microchip Rules Explained</h1>

      <div class="info-box">
        <p><strong>UPDATED NOVEMBER 11, 2026 · COUNTY ENFORCEMENT NOTICE</strong></p>
        <h2>Field animal licensing checks scheduled to begin November 12</h2>
        <p>The Los Angeles County Department of Animal Care and Control announced that officers will begin field animal licensing efforts in West Covina ZIP codes <strong>91791 and 91792</strong> on Thursday, November 12, 2026. The county says its checks address pet licensing, spay/neuter, microchipping, and rabies-vaccination requirements.</p>
        <p>Residents who are not compliant may face applicable licensing charges, delinquency fees, and a <strong>$40 field enforcement fee</strong>. This is a county-announced start date, not a claim that every home will be visited that day.</p>
        <p><strong>What residents can do:</strong> Check whether pet licenses are current, review vaccination and microchip records, and ask the county about exemptions and low-cost assistance. The county lists microchip implantation at $7.50 through its low-cost clinics and offers qualifying spay/neuter assistance.</p>
        <p><strong>Official sources:</strong> <a href="https://animalcare.lacounty.gov/news/field-animal-licensing-efforts-in-unincorporated-acton-agua-dulce-rowland-heights-walnut-and-west-covina/">October 29 county announcement</a> · <a href="https://animalcare.lacounty.gov/licensing/">Licensing information and renewals</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Still to verify:</strong> Exact enforcement routes, how many West Covina households will be contacted, and city-specific exemptions.</p>
      </div>
      <ArticleSummary text="West Covina pet owners are asking what the City's new spay, neuter, and microchip rules mean, who is affected, and what resources will be available." />

      <p><strong>Status:</strong> Developing Story</p>

